@startuml
' Figure 7.2 – Class diagram, Sprint 7: comment likes, blocks and saves
' Three new tables (FR-60, FR-62, FR-63) and the two notification types they emit.
' User, Article and Comment appear with only the attributes the new classes use.
!include ../_style.puml
skinparam nodesep 18
skinparam ranksep 45
hide circle
hide methods

class User {
  - id : UUID
  - username : String
}
class Article {
  - id : UUID
  - title : String
  - slug : String
}
class Comment {
  - id : UUID
  - content : Text
}
class CommentLike {
  - id : UUID
  - createdAt : Timestamp
}
class Block {
  - id : UUID
  - createdAt : Timestamp
}
class Save {
  - id : UUID
  - createdAt : Timestamp
}
class Notification {
  - type : NotificationType
  - data : Json
  - isRead : Boolean
}

class NotificationType <<Enumeration>> {
  ...
  comment_like
  save
}

User "1" -- "0..*" CommentLike : gives
Comment "1" -- "0..*" CommentLike : receives
User "1" -- "0..*" Block : blocker
User "1" -- "0..*" Block : blocked
User "1" -- "0..*" Save : keeps
Article "1" -- "0..*" Save : is saved in
User "1" -- "0..*" Notification : receives
Article "1" -- "0..*" Comment : carries

note as N1
  Each table carries a unique constraint on its pair —
  (user, comment), (blocker, blocked), (user, article) —
  so a repeated request is a no-op, and a notification is
  emitted only when a row was actually inserted.
  blocks also has a CHECK: no one can block themselves.
end note

note as N2
  A block row is directional, but every read treats it
  both ways: a block in either direction hides the two
  accounts from each other.
end note
@enduml
